<p class="meta">2026-06-11</p>

# Trying to train a tiny world model

I wanted to find out what the simplest world model that I could train was. I am a member of the permanent underclass, so all the compute I have to offer is the M4 MacBook I have with its 48GB of unified memory.

## The world is a 10×10×4 tensor

I think Snake is a good candidate because it's mostly deterministic and easy to represent as a tensor. The most intuitive representation for me is the 10×10 grid itself, where every cell is exactly one of four things: `empty / body / head / food`. That way a frame is just a 10×10 grid of 4-way labels. One-hot those labels and you get a 10×10×4 tensor. That's the whole world. 400 numbers, all of them 0 or 1.

<TensorFig />

Given a state and an action there is exactly one correct next state. The one exception is the food: when the snake eats, the next food spawns on a random empty cell. Remember that. It's kinda the point of this whole blog post, outside of how easy it is to get started with world models.

## Grug architecture

I wanted the simplest thing that could possibly work, very much in the spirit of [grug-brained engineering](https://grugbrain.dev/): an MLP. Flatten the frame (400 numbers), tack on a one-hot action (4 more), push it through four hidden layers, and read out 400 logits, a little 4-way classification for every cell of the next frame. Train with cross-entropy. 1,200,528 parameters. My brain is smooth, so I wanna be able to understand what is happening if it breaks.

<ArchFig />

The whole thing fits in a screenshot:

```python
class SnakeWorldModel(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()
        layers, in_dim = [], 404            # 400 board + 4 action
        for _ in range(4):
            layers += [nn.Linear(in_dim, 512), nn.ReLU()]
            in_dim = 512
        layers.append(nn.Linear(512, 400))  # 4 logits per cell
        self.net = nn.Sequential(*layers)

    def forward(self, obs, action):
        x = torch.cat([obs.flatten(1), action], dim=1)
        return self.net(x).view(-1, 4, 10, 10)
```

## Training data is cheap

A training example is a transition: `(frame, action) → next frame`. We can generate them by writing a snake simulator and running a greedy algorithm over it. To be sure we're learning a wider distribution, not just the greedy policy, we sprinkle in some randomly selected moves here and there. And then we also do some deduplication because that's relatively easy: hash every `(frame, action)` pair and throw out duplicates. 2M *unique* transitions in the final dataset.

## How do we measure the quality of this world model?

I think the most straightforward thing is the per-cell accuracy. We have a hundred cells here, so basically getting any cell right contributes 1% to this accuracy. The problem is that early on in the trajectory the board is like 95% just empty, so a policy or a model that just predicts nothing everywhere is already scoring in the upper 90s. This metric is kind of useless. So Fable suggested these four other metrics, which I agree are better:

- **active-cell accuracy**: only grade the cells that should have something in them (snake + food). The all-empty model gets exactly 0%.
- **head accuracy**: is the head in the right cell?
- **exact-frame match**: all 100 cells right.
- **model rollouts**: feed the model its own predictions for 30 steps and measure how long the predicted head stays on the real head.

## Try to get this model to learn something

So the first thing was trying to figure out how to get this thing to learn something coherent. Before we get too fancy, we could just try scaling three axes:

- how much data we have
- how much compute we used to train
- how many parameters our model is going to be

**Data × compute.** The loss surface slopes pretty smoothly towards wanting more data. Compute seems to have minimal impact until we reach a certain data threshold, and then loss kind of steeply falls off. Another thing that's quite interesting is looking at the active cell accuracy, which really picks up around a data set of 2,000 transitions.

<img src="{base}/plots/scaling_surface.png" alt="3D wireframe surfaces: loss and active-cell accuracy over data and epochs" />

**More data.** Loss keeps falling as you add transitions: head accuracy, model rollouts, all of it.

<img src="{base}/plots/scaling_data.png" alt="held-out loss (log-log) and accuracy metrics vs training data size" />

**Parameters.** Didn't really get much out of scaling the model. I think it just makes it more susceptible to overfitting, and the larger model actually did worse on the held-out set.

<img src="{base}/plots/scaling_params.png" alt="accuracy metrics vs parameter count at 16k transitions" class="half" />

The final recipe: 2M transitions, 1.2M parameters, 100 epochs at batch 2048, about six minutes on a laptop GPU.

| | |
|---|---|
| held-out loss | 0.0069 |
| active-cell accuracy | 98.0% |
| head accuracy | 99.0% |
| exact-frame match | 87.0% |
| 30-step model rollout, head on track | 96.2% |
| mean steps before divergence | 23.3 |

## Run the model forward

Both grids start from the same opening. The left is the real environment. The right is the model running inference one step at a time: after step 0 it only ever sees its own predictions.

<Demo />

## The one thing it can't predict

You might have noticed that after the snake eats the food, the model never predicts where the next food is going to spawn. It actually just predicts that none of the cells will have the food.

This isn't really a learning failure. I ran the actual exported checkpoint on a transition where the snake has just eaten. Here is the final `4×10×10` probability tensor the model produces.

<FoodFailureFig />

By making the final layer 400 logits, I basically turned the problem into 100 separate cells, each doing its own little 4-way classification. So the model is effectively saying:

<div class="math">
	<span>P(s<sub>next</sub> | s, a)</span>
	<span>≈</span>
	<span>P(cell<sub>1</sub> | s, a) × P(cell<sub>2</sub> | s, a) × ... × P(cell<sub>100</sub> | s, a)</span>
</div>

or, more compactly:

<div class="math">
	<span>P(s<sub>next</sub> | s, a)</span>
	<span>≈</span>
	<span>
		<span class="prod">
			<span class="prod-limit">100</span>
			<span class="prod-symbol">∏</span>
			<span class="prod-limit">i=1</span>
		</span>
		P(cell<sub>i</sub> | s, a)
	</span>
</div>

That assumption is mostly fine when each next cell's state can be deterministically derived from the current state and action, without depending on the predicted state of the other next cells. If the head moves right, one specific cell becomes head, another becomes body, and the tail maybe disappears. Local classification can fake that pretty well.

Food is where the assumption breaks. Predicting the next state right after the snake eats is stochastic, because the food has to respawn somewhere. The next food location is not 100 independent decisions. It is one joint decision over the empty cells. The thing we actually want is a distribution over whole boards:

<div class="math">
	<span>P(s<sub>next</sub> | s, a)</span>
</div>

where the model can assign probability to "food is in exactly this one empty cell, and nowhere else." The current head can't naturally say that. It can only say "this cell has a little food probability" 100 times.

If we tried to make the final layer predict whole board states directly, each class would correspond to one possible board. With 100 cells and 4 possible labels per cell, that would be `4^100` classes, which is around `1.6 × 10^60`. Most of those boards are invalid, but the space is still exponential. That is not feasible, even if I were not GPU impoverished.

Since the model cannot know where the food will go next, it learns the marginal answer: each plausible empty cell gets a little bit of food probability. If there are 90-ish empty cells, each one only gets around `1/90`. Then my "decoder" takes the argmax independently at every cell. Empty/body/head dominate locally, so they beat food everywhere, and the food disappears.

I think this is the actual limitation of this architecture. The thing we need is a better factorization of the next-board distribution.

One option would be to hand-design the factorization: first predict whether food should spawn somewhere else, then predict where the food should go. That would probably work for Snake. But it also feels like cheating, because the solution is not generic enough. I would be injecting too much specific knowledge about this environment directly into the model.

A more general factorization is to model the board autoregressively, as a sequence of cells. Instead of predicting all 100 cells independently, turn the board into a sequence and predict one cell at a time. Each step is still only a 4-way classification, but later cells can condition on earlier cells. That gives the model a way to represent global constraints like "there should be exactly one food" without me hard-coding a special food-spawn head.

<AutoregressiveFig />

The board becomes a line of tokens, and the model no longer has to pretend all 100 output cells are independent. That feels like the follow-up.

